Q: Overriding a class member does not override a base class member A: <p><strong>There are several issues in your code:</strong></p>
<ol>
<li><p>In order to <code>override</code> the method <code>toString</code> in <code>Rectangle</code>:<br />
(1) It must be marked as <code>virtual</code> in the base class <code>Shape</code>.<br />
(2) It must have the same signature as in <code>Shape</code> - so either both or none are <code>const</code>.</p>
<p><strong>Note</strong> that <code>Shape::toString</code> is not virtual by default and as long as that is true <code>Rectangle::toString</code> can only hide instead of override it.</p>
</li>
<li><p>You cannot concatenate different types arbitrarily in order to create a <code>std::string</code>. It works well if the first is an <code>std::string</code> and the rest are convertible to one (and <code>double</code> like <code>width</code> is not). You can use <a href="https://en.cppreference.com/w/cpp/string/basic_string/to_string" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>std::to_string</code></a> (with types that are supported).<br />
A possible fix for <code>Rectangle::toString</code>:</p>
<pre><code>return std::string(&quot;Rectangle[width = &quot;) + std::to_string(width) + &quot;height = &quot; + std::to_string(height) + &quot;]&quot;;
</code></pre>
<p><strong>Note:</strong> in the case of <code>Shape::toString</code> since <code>color</code> is a <code>std::string</code> you can omit the first explicit conversion to <code>std::string</code>. But keeping it makes the code safer e.g. if the type of <code>color</code> changes to <code>int</code> (and also more consistent with <code>Rectangle::toString</code>).</p>
</li>
<li><p>In the constructors it is better to accept the <code>std::string</code> parameter by <code>const&amp;</code> to avoid a copy.</p>
</li>
<li><p>Adding <code>using namespace std;</code> is not recommended.<br />
See: <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/questions/1452721/whats-the-problem-with-using-namespace-std">What's the problem with &quot;using namespace std;&quot;?</a>.</p>
</li>
</ol>
<p><strong>Fixed version:</strong></p>
<pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;
#include &lt;string&gt;

class Shape
{
protected:
    std::string color;
    bool filled;
public:
    Shape() {
        color = &quot;white&quot;;
        filled = false;
    }

    Shape(std::string const &amp; c, bool f) {
        color = c;
        filled = f;
    }

     virtual std::string toString() const {
         return std::string(&quot;Shape[color= &quot;) + color + &quot;filled = &quot; + (filled ? &quot;true&quot; : &quot;false&quot;) + &quot;]&quot;;
    }
};

class Rectangle : public Shape
{
protected:
    double width;
    double height;
public:
    Rectangle() {
        width = 1.0;
        height = 1.0;
    }
    Rectangle(std::string const &amp; c, bool f, double w, double h) :Shape(c , f) {
        width = w;
        height = h;
    }

    std::string toString() const override {
        return std::string(&quot;Rectangle[width = &quot;) + std::to_string(width) + &quot;height = &quot; + std::to_string(height) + &quot;]&quot;;
    }
};
</code></pre>
<p><kbd><a href="https://godbolt.org/z/MdTMvPjz9" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Live demo</a></kbd></p>

Q: Best way to handle similar type of function without writing them again in C++ A: <blockquote>
<p>Is there any way I can modify BigFunctionthat accommodates all types of funcs?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Yes, you can write a variadic function template as shown below:</p>
<pre><code>template&lt;typename... T&gt; double BigFunction(double (*func)(T...), double var)
{
  //other code here
  double var3 = func(var2);
  return var3;
}
</code></pre>
<hr />
<p>Another way is to pass a lambda.</p>

Q: Is this a bug in Devel::Cover code coverage? A: <p>Yes, you should report that as a bug in <code>Devel::Cover</code>.  If I recode your sub, I get 100%:</p>
<pre><code>sub defined_values{
    my ($a,$b) = @_;

    if( defined $a and defined $b ){
        return 'defined';
    }
    return 'not defined';
}
</code></pre>
<p><a href="https://github.com/pjcj/Devel--Cover/pull/165" rel="nofollow noreferrer">This issue may be related.</a></p>
